    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 2 (Questions 7&ndash;12)</div>\n<p>Chemical mutagens damage DNA through discrete chemical adducts that dictate cellular repair mechanisms and transgenerational heritability. Alkylating agents are electrophilic compounds that transfer alkyl groups to nucleophilic nitrogen and oxygen atoms within DNA bases. Monofunctional alkylating agents, such as temozolomide (TMZ) and ethyl methanesulfonate (EMS), transfer a single methyl or ethyl adduct. The most premutagenic lesion produced is <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-methylguanine (<em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG). During DNA replication, replicative DNA polymerases mispair <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG with thymine instead of cytosine, generating permanent G:C&rarr;A:T transition mutations after two rounds of synthesis. This lesion is directly reversed by <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT), a suicide enzyme that transfers the alkyl group irreversibly to an active-site cysteine, triggering its own ubiquitination and degradation.</p>\n<p>In contrast, bifunctional alkylating agents, such as nitrogen mustards (mechlorethamine, melphalan, and cyclophosphamide metabolites), possess two electrophilic 2-chloroethyl arms. In aqueous solution, each arm undergoes intramolecular cyclization into a highly reactive aziridinium ion that attacks the nucleophilic <em>N</em><sup>7</sup> position of guanine. When both arms react with opposing guanine bases on opposite strands across the minor or major groove, a covalent interstrand cross-link (ICL) is formed. ICLs physically tether the two strands together, completely blocking replication fork progression and RNA transcription elongation. Cellular resolution of ICLs requires a coordinated cascade involving the Fanconi anemia complex, dual structure-specific endonucleolytic unhooking, translesion DNA synthesis, and homologous recombination.</p>\n<p>The evolutionary and clinical impact of chemical DNA lesions depends fundamentally on whether mutations occur in somatic tissues or the germline. Somatic mutations are confined to non-reproductive cells; while capable of initiating neoplastic transformation in the treated individual, they cannot be passed to future offspring. Conversely, germline mutations occur in gametocytes or gamete precursors (such as spermatogonial stem cells and primary oocytes); these alterations are incorporated into the zygotic genome, entering every cell of subsequent generations and following Mendelian transmission.</p>\n<p>Researchers treated human fibroblast lines (WT vs <em>MGMT</em>-deficient) and murine spermatogonial stem cells with vehicle, 50 &mu;M TMZ, or 10 &mu;M mechlorethamine (HN2). DNA damage was quantified via alkaline comet assays measuring Olive tail moment (OTM) and cross-link index (CLI). Heritable transmission of mutations was evaluated by genome sequencing of F1 offspring (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 DNA damage metrics, MGMT enzyme activity, and transgenerational mutation frequencies following chemical mutagen exposure</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Cell Type &amp; Exposure</th><th>Agent Class</th><th>Alkaline Comet Tail Moment (OTM)</th><th>Cross-Link Index (CLI, %)</th><th>MGMT Activity (fmol/mg)</th><th>F1 Mutation Rate / Locus (&times;10<sup>&minus;5</sup>)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Fibroblasts (WT) + Vehicle</td><td>None</td><td>1.4 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>142.5 &plusmn; 8.4</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fibroblasts (WT) + TMZ</td><td>Monofunctional</td><td>16.8 &plusmn; 1.5</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>22.1 &plusmn; 3.6</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fibroblasts (<em>MGMT</em><sup>&minus;/&minus;</sup>) + TMZ</td><td>Monofunctional</td><td>38.6 &plusmn; 2.9</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 2.0</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fibroblasts (WT) + Mechlorethamine</td><td>Bifunctional</td><td>3.1 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>64.8 &plusmn; 4.2</td><td>139.8 &plusmn; 7.9</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Spermatogonia + Vehicle</td><td>None</td><td>1.2 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>128.4 &plusmn; 6.7</td><td>0.8 &plusmn; 0.1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Spermatogonia + TMZ</td><td>Monofunctional</td><td>14.5 &plusmn; 1.2</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>18.6 &plusmn; 2.4</td><td>14.2 &plusmn; 1.5</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Spermatogonia + Mechlorethamine</td><td>Bifunctional</td><td>2.8 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>59.2 &plusmn; 3.8</td><td>124.1 &plusmn; 7.2</td><td>31.6 &plusmn; 2.8</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
