        "e": "<strong>Mechanism of O6-methylguanine mutagenesis and transition mutations (Topic 614):</strong> Temozolomide and other monofunctional alkylating agents transfer a methyl group to the oxygen atom at position 6 of guanine, yielding <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-methylguanine (<em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG). In unmodified guanine, the <em>O</em><sup>6</sup> atom acts as a hydrogen bond acceptor while the <em>N</em><sup>1</sup> atom serves as a hydrogen bond donor, pairing specifically with cytosine. Methylation of the <em>O</em><sup>6</sup> oxygen disrupts this geometry: it fixes the guanine base in a tautomeric state that preferentially forms two stable Watson-Crick-like hydrogen bonds with **thymine** rather than cytosine. During the first round of DNA replication, DNA polymerases insert deoxythymidine monophosphate (dTMP) opposite template <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG. In the subsequent second round of replication, the newly incorporated thymine serves as a normal template, pairing with adenine. Consequently, the original $G:C$ base pair is permanently converted into an $A:T$ base pair. Because a purine:pyrimidine pair is replaced by another purine:pyrimidine pair ($G \rightarrow A$ and $C \rightarrow T$), this mutation is classified as a **transition** mutation.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes a transversion mutation ($G:C \rightarrow C:G$), but <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG pairs with thymine (a pyrimidine) to yield a transition mutation, not adenine.<br>&bull; B describes normal Watson-Crick pairing with cytosine and frameshifts; <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG causes base mispairing and base substitution, not insertions/deletions.<br>&bull; D is factually incorrect; <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG does form two functional hydrogen bonds with thymine, allowing polymerases to replicate across the lesion rather than permanently arresting."
