        "e": "<strong>Direct reversal mechanism and suicide kinetics of MGMT (Topic 614):</strong> Standard enzymes function catalytically: they lower the activation energy of a chemical reaction without undergoing permanent modification, emerging unchanged to turn over thousands of substrate molecules. In contrast, <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT) is a **stoichiometric, single-turnover suicide enzyme**. During the direct repair of <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-alkylguanine lesions, an invariant active-site cysteine (Cys145 in human MGMT) mounts a nucleophilic attack on the methyl group attached to the <em>O</em><sup>6</sup> position of guanine. The methyl group is transferred **covalently and irreversibly** to the cysteine thiol, generating an <em>S</em>-methylcysteine derivative and restoring unmodified guanine. This modification permanently inactivates the enzyme because cells possess no mechanism to de-methylate Cys145. Alkylation induces a conformational change that destabilizes the protein and exposes an internal degron, targeting the inactivated MGMT molecule for rapid polyubiquitination and degradation by the 26S proteasome. Thus, one molecule of MGMT repairs exactly one <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG lesion ($1:1$ stoichiometry), explaining why MGMT activity is rapidly exhausted following temozolomide exposure (plunging from 142.5 to 22.1 fmol/mg in Table 1).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes the action of AP endonuclease, which cleaves the phosphodiester backbone; MGMT repairs the base directly without cleaving the backbone and does not release suicidal peptides.<br>&bull; C is factually incorrect; MGMT-mediated direct methyl transfer is entirely ATP-independent.<br>&bull; D is a Directional Inversion / Compartment trap; ubiquitination occurs *after* the enzyme repairs an alkylated base in the nucleus, not prior to DNA binding, and is mediated by nuclear/cytosolic proteasomal ligases."
