        "a": 2,
        "e": "<strong>Futile mismatch repair cycles at unrepaired O6-MeG lesions (Topics 614 & 610):</strong> In wild-type cells with active MGMT, <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-methylguanine adducts are rapidly reversed before DNA replication occurs. In <em>MGMT</em>-deficient fibroblasts (or tumor cells with promoter-hypermethylated <em>MGMT</em>), <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG lesions persist into S phase. Replicative polymerases insert thymine opposite <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG. The resulting $O^6\text{-MeG}:T$ mispair is detected by the post-replicative mismatch repair (MMR) machinery (MutS&alpha; / MSH2-MSH6). MMR recognizes the distortion and excises the newly synthesized daughter strand containing the thymine, leaving a single-strand gap. When DNA polymerase resynthesizes the gap, it once again encounters the unrepairable template <em>O</em><sup>6</sup>-MeG and reinserters thymine. MMR recognizes the mispair again, initiating another round of excision. This repetitive, unresolvable process—termed a **futile MMR cycle**—generates persistent single-strand gaps and double-strand breaks at replication forks. Under alkaline comet conditions, these extensive strand breaks allow fragmented DNA to migrate rapidly, driving the Olive tail moment up from 16.8 in WT to 38.6 in *MGMT*<sup>&minus;/&minus;</sup> cells.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Pathway Misattribution trap; elevated comet tail moments reflect unrepaired alkylation-induced MMR nicks, not topoisomerase II transcriptional upregulation.<br>&bull; B is false because TMZ is a *monofunctional* alkylating agent that cannot form interstrand cross-links regardless of MGMT status.<br>&bull; D is a Mechanism Misattribution trap; Ku70/80 binds double-strand ends to facilitate NHEJ ligation; it does not possess exonuclease activity that digests DNA ends."
