        "e": "<strong>Chemistry of nitrogen mustards and interstrand cross-link formation (Topic 614):</strong> Bifunctional nitrogen mustards contain a central nitrogen atom bonded to two 2-chloroethyl groups ($-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{Cl}$). In aqueous biological environments, the lone pair of electrons on the central nitrogen attacks the &beta;-carbon, displacing a chloride ion in an intramolecular nucleophilic substitution ($S_N2$) to form a strained, highly reactive, electrophilic three-membered **aziridinium ion** (ethyleneimmonium intermediate). The most nucleophilic site in double-stranded DNA is the *N*<sup>7</sup> position of guanine located within the major groove. The aziridinium ring is attacked by the *N*<sup>7</sup> nitrogen of a guanine base, covalently attaching the mustard arm. Because the molecule contains a second 2-chloroethyl group, the reaction sequence repeats: the second arm cyclizes into a second aziridinium ion that attacks the *N*<sup>7</sup> nitrogen of a second guanine residing on the **opposite strand** of the DNA duplex (most frequently at 5'-GNC-3' sequences). This forms a stable, covalent covalent bridge linking both strands—an interstrand cross-link (ICL)—which completely prevents denaturation and strand separation.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes intercalating agents (such as doxorubicin, daunorubicin, or ethidium bromide), which slip between base pairs non-covalently rather than forming bifunctional covalent cross-links.<br>&bull; B describes radiomimetic antibiotics (like bleomycin) or epoxide metabolites that attack deoxyribose to induce free-radical strand breaks.<br>&bull; C describes photoactivated psoralens or synthetic azido cross-linkers that require UV light excitation, unlike nitrogen mustards which react spontaneously."
