    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>Monogenic human diseases illustrate foundational Mendelian inheritance paradigms governed by gene dosage, molecular dominance, and cellular pathogenesis. Achondroplasia, the most frequent cause of disproportionate dwarfism, follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern with complete penetrance. Approximately 99% of cases result from a specific point mutation in the <em>FGFR3</em> gene on chromosome 4p16.3—a c.1138G&rarr;A transition causing a Gly380Arg substitution in the transmembrane domain of fibroblast growth factor receptor 3 (FGFR3). Under normal physiology, FGFR3 functions as a receptor tyrosine kinase that negatively regulates endochondral ossification by activating STAT1 and MAPK signaling in epiphyseal growth plate chondrocytes. The Gly380Arg substitution stabilizes ligand-independent receptor homodimerization and constitutive trans-autophosphorylation, excessively arresting chondrocyte proliferation. While heterozygotes (<em>FGFR3</em><sup>G380R/+</sup>) exhibit rhizomelic shortening and macrocephaly with normal life expectancy, homozygous individuals (<em>FGFR3</em><sup>G380R/G380R</sup>) suffer severe thoracic cage hypoplasia that is invariably lethal perinatally. Remarkably, over 80% of achondroplasia cases arise from <em>de novo</em> germline mutations strongly linked to advanced paternal age.</p>\n<p>Conversely, cystic fibrosis (CF) is the classic autosomal recessive multiorgan disorder, affecting approximately 1 in 2,500 live births in Northern European populations. CF is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the <em>CFTR</em> gene on chromosome 7q31.2, encoding an ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter-class chloride and bicarbonate channel. The most prevalent mutant allele, &Delta;F508 (c.1521_1523delCTT), involves an in-frame deletion of phenylalanine at codon 508 in the first nucleotide-binding domain (NBD1). This deletion causes protein misfolding in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). ER-associated quality control chaperones retain the mutant protein, preventing core-glycosylated immature CFTR (Band B, ~140 kDa) from trafficking to the Golgi for complex glycosylation (Band C, ~170 kDa), instead routing it to the 26S proteasome for degradation. Lack of apical membrane CFTR disrupts epithelial chloride secretion and deregulates epithelial sodium channels (ENaC), producing hyperviscous mucus in the lungs and pancreas, accompanied by elevated sweat chloride concentrations.</p>\n<p>A medical genetics clinic evaluated five individuals representing diverse combinations of achondroplasia and CFTR status. Chondrocyte FGFR3 autophosphorylation, nasal epithelial CFTR maturation, and sweat chloride were quantified (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 FGFR3 activation, CFTR biochemical maturation, and sweat chloride concentrations across patient cohorts</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Individual &amp; Genotype</th><th>Inheritance Classification</th><th>FGFR3 Tyrosine Phosphorylation (%)</th><th>Mature Band C CFTR (% Total)</th><th>Sweat Chloride (mmol/L)</th><th>Clinical Phenotype</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Individual 1: <em>FGFR3</em><sup>+/+</sup>, <em>CFTR</em><sup>+/+</sup></td><td>Wild-Type Control</td><td>100 &plusmn; 6%</td><td>78.4 &plusmn; 3.5%</td><td>18.2 &plusmn; 2.1</td><td>Unaffected Control</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Individual 2: <em>FGFR3</em><sup>G380R/+</sup>, <em>CFTR</em><sup>+/+</sup></td><td>Autosomal Dominant Heterozygote</td><td>345 &plusmn; 18%</td><td>76.8 &plusmn; 3.2%</td><td>19.5 &plusmn; 2.4</td><td>Achondroplasia</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Individual 3: <em>FGFR3</em><sup>+/+</sup>, <em>CFTR</em><sup>&Delta;F508/+</sup></td><td>Autosomal Recessive Carrier</td><td>98 &plusmn; 5%</td><td>42.1 &plusmn; 2.8%</td><td>24.3 &plusmn; 2.6</td><td>Asymptomatic Carrier</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Individual 4: <em>FGFR3</em><sup>+/+</sup>, <em>CFTR</em><sup>&Delta;F508/&Delta;F508</sup></td><td>Autosomal Recessive Homozygote</td><td>104 &plusmn; 7%</td><td>&lt; 2.0%</td><td>106.8 &plusmn; 6.4</td><td>Cystic Fibrosis</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Individual 5: <em>FGFR3</em><sup>G380R/+</sup>, <em>CFTR</em><sup>&Delta;F508/+</sup></td><td>Double Heterozygote</td><td>338 &plusmn; 16%</td><td>44.5 &plusmn; 2.9%</td><td>23.8 &plusmn; 2.5</td><td>Achondroplasia; CF Carrier</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
