        "a": 0,
        "e": "<strong>Lethality of homozygous dominant alleles and conditional probability in achondroplasia (Topic 616):</strong> Both parents have achondroplasia; because homozygous achondroplasia is neonatally lethal, living individuals with achondroplasia are obligate heterozygotes ($Aa$). When two heterozygous individuals mate ($Aa \times Aa$), the resulting Punnett square yields the classic Mendelian genotypic ratio of $1\\ AA : 2\\ Aa : 1\\ aa$:<br>&bull; $1/4\\ AA$ (homozygous dominant): exhibits severe, catastrophic thoracic dysplasia that causes neonatal death.<br>&bull; $2/4\\ Aa$ (heterozygous): exhibits viable achondroplasia.<br>&bull; $1/4\\ aa$ (homozygous recessive): exhibits normal stature.<br>Because the question specifically asks for the probability among **living newborn children**, the lethal $AA$ genotype is eliminated from the denominator. The remaining viable outcomes comprise $2\\ Aa + 1\\ aa = 3$ total viable possibilities. Of these 3 surviving outcomes, 2 possess achondroplasia ($Aa$). Thus, the conditional probability is $2/3$ (66.7%).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B fails to account for the perinatal lethality of the homozygous dominant ($AA$) genotype; while $3/4$ of conceptions inherit a mutant allele, $AA$ conceptuses do not survive.<br>&bull; C represents the probability of an affected child from a cross between a heterozygous affected individual and an unaffected individual ($Aa \times aa$), not two affected parents.<br>&bull; D represents the probability of a homozygous recessive unaffected child ($aa$) before eliminating lethal genotypes, and incorrectly claims incomplete penetrance (achondroplasia has 100% penetrance)."
