        "e": "<strong>CFTR delta-F508 misfolding, ER-associated degradation (ERAD), and trafficking failure (Topic 617):</strong> Normal CFTR is synthesized and cotranslationally translocated into the rough endoplasmic reticulum (ER), where it receives core $N$-linked oligosaccharides to yield immature **Band B** (~140 kDa). Correctly folded CFTR then buds in COPII vesicles to the Golgi apparatus, where glycosyltransferases modify the glycans into complex, high-molecular-weight forms to produce mature **Band C** (~170 kDa), which is delivered to the apical plasma membrane. The &Delta;F508 mutation is an in-frame three-base deletion (c.1521_1523delCTT) that removes a single phenylalanine residue in nucleotide-binding domain 1 (NBD1). This deletion disrupts domain-domain folding thermodynamics. ER chaperone surveillance systems (such as Hsp70 and calnexin) recognize the exposed hydrophobic patches of misfolded &Delta;F508-CFTR and retain it in the ER. The mutant protein is then retrotranslocated through the translocon into the cytosol, polyubiquitinated by E3 ubiquitin ligases, and degraded by the 26S proteasome (ERAD). Because &Delta;F508-CFTR never reaches the Golgi apparatus, mature Band C is completely undetectable (< 2.0% in Table 1).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A confuses an in-frame 3-bp deletion with a nonsense mutation; &Delta;F508 removes a single codon (Phe) without causing a frameshift or generating a premature stop codon.<br>&bull; B is false because the signal anchor domains that direct cotranslational translocation into the ER are located in the transmembrane domains, not at codon 508.<br>&bull; D is a Compartment / Pathway Misattribution trap; the arrest occurs earlier in the endoplasmic reticulum (preventing Golgi entry), not in trans-Golgi vesicular exit."
