        "e": "<strong>Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium and Mendelian recurrence risk calculation (Topic 617):</strong> To calculate the probability that the child will be affected with an autosomal recessive disease ($aa$), we multiply: $P(\text{affected}) = P(\text{father is carrier}) \times P(\text{mother is carrier}) \times P(\text{two carriers transmit mutant allele})$.<br>1. **Father's carrier probability:** The man has an affected sister ($aa$), which proves that both of his parents are unaffected obligate carriers ($Aa \times Aa$). The man himself is healthy (unaffected); therefore, he cannot have genotype $aa$. Among the three remaining viable genotypes ($1\\ AA : 2\\ Aa$), the conditional probability that he is a carrier ($Aa$) is **$2/3$**.<br>2. **Mother's carrier probability:** CF prevalence is $q^2 = 1/2500$. Thus, the mutant allele frequency is $q = \\sqrt{1/2500} = 1/50$. The wild-type allele frequency is $p = 1 - q = 49/50 \u0007pprox 1$. Carrier frequency in the general population is $2pq \u0007pprox 2 \times 1 \times (1/50) = 2/50 =$ **$1/25$**.<br>3. **Transmission probability:** If both parents are carriers ($Aa \times Aa$), the probability they will conceive an affected child ($aa$) is **$1/4$**.<br>Multiplying these independent probabilities:<br>$$P(\text{affected child}) = \frac{2}{3} \times \frac{1}{25} \times \frac{1}{4} = \frac{2}{300} = \frac{1}{150}$$<br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A incorrectly sets the father's carrier risk to $1/2$ (a common trap confusing general offspring risk with conditional risk given that he is unaffected: $2/3$).<br>&bull; C incorrectly sets the father's carrier risk to $1/4$ (confusing carrier probability with the probability of having an affected child) and miscalculates carrier frequency.<br>&bull; D miscalculates the general population carrier frequency as $1/100$ instead of $2pq = 1/25$."
