        "e": "<strong>Distinction between penetrance and variable expressivity (Topic 621):</strong> Penetrance and expressivity are essential concepts in medical genetics that describe the relationship between genotype and phenotype:<br>&bull; **Penetrance:** An **all-or-none statistical parameter** at the population level. It is the percentage of individuals carrying a particular disease-causing genotype who exhibit *any* detectable phenotypic manifestation of the condition. For example, at 22 &deg;C, 100% of individuals with the *N* allele show dysplasia (complete penetrance). At 30 &deg;C, only 70% of *N* carriers show lesions (reduced/incomplete penetrance).<br>&bull; **Variable Expressivity:** An **individual-level parameter** describing the qualitative or quantitative *severity*, intensity, or spectrum of clinical symptoms observed among those individuals who do manifest the phenotype. In Table 1, affected individuals exhibit varying severity (mean expressivity scores around 7.6 to 7.9, with standard deviations reflecting individual variation in lesion burden despite having the identical *N* allele).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A completely inverts the definitions of penetrance and expressivity (a classic AAMC Directional Inversion trap).<br>&bull; B is false because both penetrance and variable expressivity apply to autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, and X-linked conditions.<br>&bull; D is an Overgeneralization trap; while temperature modulates penetrance in this model, penetrance and expressivity are influenced by complex combinations of modifier genes, epigenetics, and environmental factors, not exclusively temperature or copy number."
